    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d298">

                <pb facs="0485" n="445" sameAs="frus:frus1865p1/pg445"/>

                <head>Mr. Hunter to Mr. Adams</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1497.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1865-07-31"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >July</hi> 31, 1865</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your interesting despatch of the 13th
                    instant, No. 1014, relative to the feeling evinced towards the United States
                    during the recent elections which have taken place in England, has been
                    received. Those elections, so far as they indicate sympathy with our national
                    cause and promise progress in liberal ideas, are highly gratifying, and your
                    remarks upon the dependence of those ideas upon the course of events here are
                    judicious, and I trust will be duly appreciated.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>W. HUNTER, <hi rendition="#italic">Acting
                    Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams,</hi> Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c. London.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
